  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nActive weather continues into Thursday as the system pushes through \nNebraska. Confidence is increasing in QPF amounts with most \nlocations seeing at least a half inch of rainfall. Some isolated \nlocations under some of the heavier showers or thunderstorms could \neven see over an inch. The latest probabilities support this as the \nprobability of seeing over an inch is 80 percent or higher across \nthe entire forecast area, and the probability of seeing over 1 inch \nis over 50 percent for areas across the Sandhills and into central \nNebraska. While these rainfall totals are significant, especially \nafter several days with no rain, the longer duration of this system \nshould keep any flooding concerns at bay. Still, will be monitoring \nthe rainfall totals and flooding potential closely as the event \nunfolds. Precipitation will begin to push out of the area Thursday \nafternoon, completely exiting the area by Thursday evening. \n\nBeyond Thursday night, strong upper level ridging will return to the \nwestern US allowing for surface high pressure to build south out of \nCanada bringing a return to dry conditions through the weekend. With \nthis high, cold air advection will push in a much colder airmass \ninto the northern and central Great Plains. With 850 mb temperatures \nin the -1 to 4 C range on Friday, surface highs will struggle to \nrise out of the 50s. Highs will gradually moderate through the \nweekend, rising back into the 70s by Sunday. This milder air will \npersist through the beginning of next week.\n\nFor low temperatures, this CAA will drop overnight temperatures into \nthe upper 20s to low 30s across the Panhandle and northwest Nebraska \nwith elsewhere seeing lows in the mid 30s on Friday morning. \nWidespread low temperatures in the low to mid 30s continue into \nFriday night/Saturday morning. Given this colder airmass, frost and \nfreeze potential is likely by the end of the week. At this time, \nheadlines are not in place, but if this colder temperature trend \nremains consistent and confidence increases, they will be necessary. \nStay tuned to the latest forecasts for the most up to date \ninformation regarding frost/freeze potential.",
